//? Title: Advanced Optimizers: LAMB, LARS, Lion, Adafactor, Muon, Lookahead and SAM
//? Subtitle: Layer-wise trust ratios, sign updates, factored second moments, Newton–Schulz orthogonalised updates, slow weights and sharpness-aware gradients, with their exact theory checked and a learning-rate sweep on digits, using datamunge's nn::Optimizer, nn::Muon, nn::Lookahead, nn::sam_gradients and nn::newton_schulz_orthogonalize
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
LAMB, LARS, Lion and Adafactor change the shape of an optimizer's update rather than its scale: per layer, per sign or through a factored second moment. This report checks the four built-in rules against their closed forms, to machine precision. It adds Muon, which orthogonalises each weight matrix's update by Newton–Schulz iteration, together with two wrappers that work with any optimizer: Lookahead and sharpness-aware minimisation (SAM).
The theory checks are exact. Newton–Schulz maps each singular value through a fixed polynomial: the classical cubic converges to the orthogonal polar factor, while Muon's quintic stops at singular values between 0.68 and 1.13 after two steps. Lookahead's contraction on a quadratic equals \(1-\alpha+\alpha c^k\) to \(10^{-16}\); with an odd number of inner steps it converges even where gradient descent diverges. Adafactor's rank-one second moment is exact for a rank-one gradient, and its relative error is 0.55 already at rank two. On a loss with a sharp and a flat minimum, SAM does not move runs into the flat well. Instead, for large radii it creates a new stationary point at distance \(\rho\) from the sharp minimum.
On the digits data, in a short 500-step sweep, every method reaches 95–98% validation accuracy at its best learning rate. Only Adafactor and Muon stay above 90% over the whole 300-fold range of rates, while LAMB, LARS and Lion collapse at the larger ones.
)

\toc

> The problem

Adam and its relatives (see the report on adaptive optimizers) rescale each coordinate separately. Several later methods change the geometry of the update instead:
- LARS (You, Gitman and Ginsburg, 2017) and LAMB (You et al., 2020) scale each layer's update by the ratio of the weight norm to the update norm, so that every layer moves by the same relative amount. They were designed for very large batches.
- Lion (Chen et al., 2023), found by program search, moves every coordinate by exactly the learning rate in the direction of the sign of a momentum mix.
- Adafactor (Shazeer and Stern, 2018) stores only the row and column means of the squared gradients of a weight matrix, instead of a full second moment, to save memory.
- Muon (Jordan et al., 2024) replaces the momentum of each weight matrix by the nearest orthogonal matrix, computed approximately by a Newton–Schulz iteration.

Two further methods wrap any optimizer. Lookahead (Zhang et al., 2019) keeps slow weights that follow the optimizer's fast weights. SAM (Foret et al., 2021) evaluates the gradient at the worst point in a small neighbourhood, to favour minima whose surroundings are flat.

This report replaces the original example `examples/nn/0_foundations/0.3_optimizers_and_training_loop/advanced_optimizers.cpp`. That example checked LAMB's and LARS's relative update norms, Lion's use of the old momentum, Adafactor's factored moments on a rank-one squared gradient, the behaviour of all four with zero gradients, Adafactor's memory and a small matrix fit. All of that is kept.
Added are Muon, Lookahead, SAM and the Newton–Schulz iteration, in `datamunge/nn/advanced_variants.hpp`, together with checks of their theory and a dataset.

> The method

>> Layer-wise trust ratios: LARS and LAMB

For a layer with weights \(w\) and gradient \(g\), LARS keeps a velocity

$$
v\leftarrow\mu v+\eta\,\tau\,\frac{\lVert w\rVert}{\lVert g\rVert+\lambda\lVert w\rVert}\,(g+\lambda w),\qquad w\leftarrow w-v,
$$
\alttext(v becomes mu v plus eta times tau times the norm of w over the norm of g plus lambda times the norm of w, times g plus lambda w; w becomes w minus v.)

with trust coefficient \(\tau\). Without momentum and decay, a step moves the layer by exactly \(\eta\tau\lVert w\rVert\), a fixed fraction of its norm. LAMB applies the same idea to Adam: it computes Adam's direction \(d=\hat m/(\sqrt{\hat v}+\epsilon)+\lambda w\) and steps \(\eta\,d\,\lVert w\rVert/\lVert d\rVert\), so \(\lVert\Delta w\rVert=\eta\lVert w\rVert\).

>> Lion

Lion keeps one momentum \(m\) and updates

$$
w\leftarrow w-\eta\,\big(\mathrm{sign}(\beta_1m+(1-\beta_1)g)+\lambda w\big),\qquad m\leftarrow\beta_2m+(1-\beta_2)g.
$$
\alttext(w becomes w minus eta times the sign of beta 1 m plus 1 minus beta 1 times g, plus lambda w; then m becomes beta 2 m plus 1 minus beta 2 times g.)

The sign uses the old momentum, mixed with the new gradient by \(\beta_1\), while the stored momentum uses \(\beta_2\). Without decay every coordinate with a nonzero mix moves by exactly \(\eta\), so \(\lVert\Delta w\rVert=\eta\sqrt d\) for \(d\) coordinates. Lion stores one slot per weight, half of Adam's two.

>> Adafactor

For an \(n\times m\) weight matrix, Adafactor keeps row means \(R_i\) and column means \(C_j\) of \(G\circ G\), each an exponential average with decay \(1-t^{-0.8}\), and estimates the second moment by the rank-one matrix

$$
\hat V_{ij}=\frac{R_i\,C_j}{\tfrac1n\sum_kR_k}.
$$
\alttext(V hat i j equals R i times C j over the mean of R.)

If \(G\circ G=ab^\top\) has rank one, then \(R=a\,\bar b\) and \(C=\bar a\,b\), and \(\hat V=ab^\top\) exactly. In general \(G\circ G\) is not of rank one. If \(G=\sum_{i=1}^ru_iv_i^\top\) has rank \(r\), then \(G\circ G=\sum_{i,j}(u_i\circ u_j)(v_i\circ v_j)^\top\), and since the terms for \((i,j)\) and \((j,i)\) are equal, its rank is at most \(r(r+1)/2\). The memory is \(n+m\) floats instead of \(nm\).
The update \(G/\sqrt{\hat V}\) is divided by \(\max(1,\mathrm{RMS}/d)\) for a clipping threshold \(d\). By default the learning rate is capped at \(1/\sqrt t\) and multiplied by \(\max(\mathrm{RMS}(w),10^{-3})\).

>> Muon and Newton–Schulz orthogonalisation

Write the momentum of a weight matrix as \(M=U\Sigma V^\top\). Muon steps along the orthogonal factor \(UV^\top\), which keeps the singular vectors and sets every singular value to 1. The update then has the same size in every direction of the matrix. Bernstein and Newhouse (2024) show that this is steepest descent under the spectral norm.
An SVD at every step is expensive. Muon uses instead an odd polynomial \(p(x)=ax+bx^3+cx^5\) applied to the matrix:

$$
X\leftarrow aX+b\,(XX^\top)X+c\,(XX^\top)^2X.
$$
\alttext(X becomes a X plus b times X X transpose times X plus c times X X transpose squared times X.)

If \(X=U\Sigma V^\top\), then \((XX^\top)X=U\Sigma^3V^\top\), so one step maps every singular value \(\sigma\) to \(p(\sigma)\) and leaves \(U\) and \(V\) unchanged. Starting from \(X_0=M/\lVert M\rVert_F\), whose singular values lie in \((0,1]\), \(k\) steps give exactly \(U\,p^{(k)}(\Sigma/\lVert M\rVert_F)\,V^\top\).
The classical choice \(p(x)=\tfrac32x-\tfrac12x^3\) (Björck and Bowie, 1971) converges to 1 for every \(\sigma\in(0,\sqrt3)\), quadratically near 1, but grows small values only by a factor of 1.5 per step. Jordan's quintic \((3.4445,-4.7750,2.0315)\) grows them by 3.44 per step, at the price of not converging: the values end up oscillating around 1.
`nn::newton_schulz_orthogonalize` implements the iteration. `nn::Muon` keeps a Nesterov momentum \(m\leftarrow\mu m+g\), \(u=g+\mu m\), and steps \(w\leftarrow w-\eta\lambda w-\eta\sqrt{\max(1,n/m)}\,\mathrm{NS}_5(u)\). Parameters that are not matrices, such as biases, get the plain momentum update \(w\leftarrow w-\eta u\).

>> Lookahead

Lookahead keeps slow weights \(\phi\). Every \(k\) steps of the inner optimizer it sets \(\phi\leftarrow\phi+\alpha(\theta-\phi)\) and resets the fast weights \(\theta\) to \(\phi\). For gradient descent on \(\tfrac12hw^2\), each inner step multiplies the error by \(c=1-\eta h\). One outer cycle starts at \(\phi\), reaches \(c^k\phi\) and moves the slow weights to

$$
\phi\leftarrow(1-\alpha+\alpha c^k)\,\phi.
$$
\alttext(phi becomes 1 minus alpha plus alpha c to the k, times phi.)

The rate per inner step is \(|1-\alpha+\alpha c^k|^{1/k}\). For small \(\eta h\) it is about \(1-\alpha\eta h\), slower than gradient descent. For \(c\) near \(-1\), where gradient descent oscillates, an odd \(k\) gives \(c^k\approx-1\) and a factor near \(1-2\alpha\), which is 0 for \(\alpha=0.5\).

>> Sharpness-aware minimisation

SAM minimises \(\max_{\lVert\epsilon\rVert\le\rho}L(w+\epsilon)\). A first-order expansion gives the maximiser \(\epsilon=\rho\,g/\lVert g\rVert\) with \(g=\nabla L(w)\), and SAM uses \(\nabla L(w+\epsilon)\), treating \(\epsilon\) as a constant. `nn::sam_gradients` computes this gradient over all parameters jointly, at the cost of two forward and backward passes, and returns it for any optimizer's `step()`.
The first-order maximiser is not the true maximiser. In one dimension \(\epsilon=\rho\,\mathrm{sign}(L'(w))\), so SAM looks only uphill, a distance \(\rho\) away.

> Simple examples

>> Closed-form checks, memory and a matrix fit

The program repeats the original checks: one LAMB and one LARS step from \(w=(3,4)\) with gradient \((1,-2)\), learning rate 0.1 and trust coefficient 0.1; two Lion steps with gradients 10 and \(-0.85\); one clipped Adafactor step on a \(2\times3\) matrix with the rank-one squared gradient \((1,2,3;2,4,6)^{\circ2}\); zero gradients on a scalar, a vector and a rank-3 tensor; and the slot memory of a \(256\times128\) matrix. It adds a check of Lion's update norm over 20 random steps.
Finally it fits a \(3\times4\) matrix by mean squared error for 1500 steps, with the learning rate decayed linearly to zero, using each method and also Adam and Muon.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/advanced_basics.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Tensor;
using Kind = nn::OptimizerKind;
using dm::graphics::RGB;
const auto F64 = nn::DType::Float64;

Tensor vec(std::vector<double> v, nn::Shape s) { return nn::from_vector(v, s); }
Tensor gauss(std::int64_t r, std::int64_t c, std::uint64_t seed) {
  std::mt19937_64 rng(seed);
  std::normal_distribution<double> N;
  std::vector<double> v(static_cast<std::size_t>(r * c));
  for (auto& x : v) x = N(rng);
  return nn::from_vector(v, {r, c});
}
Tensor slot(const nn::Optimizer& o, const std::string& name) {
  for (const auto& [k, v] : o.state()) if (k == name) return v;
  throw std::runtime_error("no slot " + name);
}
long long slot_floats(const nn::Optimizer& o) {
  long long n = 0;
  for (const auto& [k, v] : o.state()) if (k != "step" && k != "nadam_product") n += v.numel();
  return n;
}

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  // 1. One step from w = (3, 4), gradient (1, -2), learning rate 0.1: the relative update norm of LAMB is the learning
  // rate itself, that of LARS the learning rate times the trust coefficient 0.1.
  std::print("| check | computed | expected |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (auto kind : {Kind::Lamb, Kind::Lars}) {
    Tensor w = vec({3, 4}, {2});
    w.requires_grad_();
    nn::OptimizerConfig cfg;
    cfg.kind = kind, cfg.momentum = 0, cfg.trust_coefficient = 0.1;
    nn::Optimizer o({w}, 0.1, cfg);
    o.step({vec({1, -2}, {2})});
    const auto v = w.to_vector<double>();
    std::print("| {} ‖Δw‖/‖w‖ after one step | {:.6f} | {} |\n", nn::optimizer_name(kind), std::hypot(v[0] - 3, v[1] - 4) / 5, kind == Kind::Lamb ? "0.1" : "0.01");
  }
  // 2. Lion picks the sign from the OLD momentum mixed with the new gradient (β1 = 0.9) and stores momentum with β2.
  {
    Tensor w = vec({1}, {1});
    w.requires_grad_();
    nn::OptimizerConfig cfg;
    cfg.kind = Kind::Lion, cfg.beta2 = 0.99;
    nn::Optimizer o({w}, 0.1, cfg);
    for (double g : {10.0, -0.85}) o.step({vec({g}, {1})});
    std::print("| Lion w after gradients 10 then −0.85 | {:.6f} | 0.8 |\n", w.item());
    std::print("| Lion stored momentum | {:.6f} | 0.0905 |\n", slot(o, "m/0").item());
    // Every Lion step moves every coordinate by exactly the learning rate, so ‖Δw‖ = η √d.
    Tensor W = gauss(16, 8, 3);
    W.requires_grad_();
    nn::Optimizer ol({W}, 0.01, cfg);
    double worst = 0;
    for (int t = 0; t < 20; ++t) {
      const Tensor before = W.detach().clone();
      ol.step({gauss(16, 8, 100 + t)});
      worst = std::max(worst, std::abs(std::sqrt(nn::sum(nn::square(W.detach() - before)).item()) - 0.01 * std::sqrt(128.0)));
    }
    std::print("| Lion: largest deviation of ‖Δw‖ from η√d over 20 random steps (16×8) | {:.1e} | 0 |\n", worst);
  }
  // 3. Adafactor on a 2×3 matrix with the rank-one squared gradient G∘G = r c^T: the factored second moment is exact.
  {
    Tensor w = nn::ones({2, 3}, F64);
    w.requires_grad_();
    nn::OptimizerConfig cfg;
    cfg.kind = Kind::Adafactor, cfg.scale_parameter = cfg.relative_step = false, cfg.update_clip = 0.5;
    nn::Optimizer o({w}, 0.1, cfg);
    o.step({vec({1, 2, 3, 2, 4, 6}, {2, 3})});
    const auto wv = w.to_vector<double>(), row = slot(o, "row/0").to_vector<double>(), col = slot(o, "col/0").to_vector<double>();
    std::print("| Adafactor weights after one clipped step (all six) | {:.6f} … {:.6f} | 0.95 |\n", *std::ranges::min_element(wv), *std::ranges::max_element(wv));
    std::print("| Adafactor row moments | {:.6f}, {:.6f} | 14/3, 56/3 |\n", row[0], row[1]);
    std::print("| Adafactor column moments | {:.4f}, {:.4f}, {:.4f} | 2.5, 10, 22.5 |\n", col[0], col[1], col[2]);
    std::print("| Adafactor slot floats for a 2×3 matrix | {} | 5 |\n", slot_floats(o));
  }
  // 4. Zero gradients on a scalar, a vector and a rank-3 tensor keep every method finite; slot memory of a 256×128 matrix.
  {
    int finite = 0, total = 0;
    for (auto kind : {Kind::Lamb, Kind::Lars, Kind::Lion, Kind::Adafactor})
      for (const nn::Shape& shape : {nn::Shape{}, nn::Shape{3}, nn::Shape{2, 3, 4}}) {
        Tensor w = nn::zeros(shape, F64);
        w.requires_grad_();
        nn::OptimizerConfig cfg;
        cfg.kind = kind;
        nn::Optimizer o({w}, 0.01, cfg);
        for (int t = 0; t < 3; ++t) o.step({nn::zeros_like(w)});
        bool ok = true;
        for (double v : w.to_vector<double>()) ok = ok && v == 0.0;
        finite += ok, ++total;
      }
    std::print("| cases with zero gradients that stay exactly at zero | {} of {} | 12 of 12 |\n", finite, total);
    for (auto kind : {Kind::Adam, Kind::Lamb, Kind::Lars, Kind::Lion, Kind::Adafactor}) {
      Tensor w = nn::zeros({256, 128}, F64);
      w.requires_grad_();
      nn::OptimizerConfig cfg;
      cfg.kind = kind;
      nn::Optimizer o({w}, 0.01, cfg);
      o.step({nn::ones_like(w)});
      std::print("| {} slot floats for a 256×128 matrix | {} | |\n", nn::optimizer_name(kind), slot_floats(o));
    }
  }

  // 5. Fit a 3×4 matrix to a target by mean squared error, 1500 steps, learning rate decayed linearly to zero. Muon and
  // Adam are added for comparison.
  std::vector<double> tv(12);
  for (int i = 0; i < 12; ++i) tv[i] = 0.3 + 0.5 * std::sin(i);
  const Tensor target = vec(tv, {3, 4});
  struct Run { std::string name; RGB color; double lr; std::vector<double> loss, rel; long long slots; };
  std::vector<Run> runs{{"Adam", {37, 99, 235}, 0.03, {}, {}, 0}, {"LAMB", {220, 38, 38}, 0.03, {}, {}, 0}, {"LARS", {202, 138, 4}, 0.1, {}, {}, 0},
                        {"Lion", {22, 163, 74}, 0.01, {}, {}, 0}, {"Adafactor", {124, 58, 237}, 0.03, {}, {}, 0}, {"Muon", {17, 24, 39}, 0.03, {}, {}, 0}};
  const int steps = 1500;
  std::print("\n| method | initial η | MSE after 100 steps | after 500 | after 1500 | persistent slot floats |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (auto& run : runs) {
    Tensor w = nn::ones({3, 4}, F64);
    w.requires_grad_();
    auto loss_of = [&] { return nn::mean(nn::square(w - target)); };
    std::shared_ptr<nn::Optimizer> o;
    std::shared_ptr<nn::Muon> mu;
    if (run.name == "Muon") {
      mu = std::make_shared<nn::Muon>(std::vector<Tensor>{w}, run.lr);
      run.slots = 12;
    } else {
      nn::OptimizerConfig cfg;
      cfg.kind = run.name == "Adam" ? Kind::Adam : run.name == "LAMB" ? Kind::Lamb : run.name == "LARS" ? Kind::Lars : run.name == "Lion" ? Kind::Lion : Kind::Adafactor;
      cfg.trust_coefficient = 0.1;
      if (cfg.kind == Kind::Lion) cfg.beta2 = 0.99;
      o = std::make_shared<nn::Optimizer>(std::vector<Tensor>{w}, run.lr, cfg);
    }
    std::vector<double> at;
    for (int t = 0; t < steps; ++t) {
      const double lr = run.lr * (1 - t / 1500.0);
      const Tensor before = w.detach().clone();
      if (mu) mu->set_learning_rate(lr), mu->minimize(loss_of());
      else o->set_learning_rate(lr), o->minimize(loss_of());
      const double l = loss_of().item();
      run.loss.push_back(std::log10(std::max(l, 1e-16)));
      run.rel.push_back(std::log10(std::max(std::sqrt(nn::sum(nn::square(w.detach() - before)).item() / nn::sum(nn::square(before)).item()), 1e-12)));
      if (t + 1 == 100 || t + 1 == 500 || t + 1 == 1500) at.push_back(l);
    }
    if (o) run.slots = slot_floats(*o);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {:.3g} | {:.3g} | {:.3g} | {} |\n", run.name, run.lr, at[0], at[1], at[2], run.slots);
  }

  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  for (int panel = 0; panel < 2; ++panel) {
    auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    for (const auto& run : runs) {
      std::vector<double> xs, ys;
      for (int t = 0; t < steps; t += 5) xs.push_back(t + 1), ys.push_back(panel == 0 ? run.loss[t] : run.rel[t]);
      pl.lines(xs, ys, run.name, run.color, 2);
    }
    if (panel == 0) pl.x_limits(0, 1530).y_limits(-16.5, 6).title("Matrix fit: loss").x_label("step").y_label("log10 mean squared error");
    else pl.x_limits(0, 1530).y_limits(-12.5, 4.5).title("Matrix fit: relative update size").x_label("step").y_label("log10 ‖Δw‖ / ‖w‖");
    fig.add(pl);
  }
  fig.size(1100, 520).fit_panels();
  fig.save("build/advanced_basics.svg");
}
```
