\image(figures/advanced_basics.svg)
// result_end

\caption(The \(3\times4\) matrix fit. Left: the loss (log scale; values below \(10^{-16}\), including exact zeros, are drawn at \(10^{-16}\)). Right: the size of each update relative to the weights (values below \(10^{-12}\) drawn at \(10^{-12}\)).)
\alttext(Left: Adafactor and Adam fall steeply to the floor of 10⁻¹⁶ by steps 200 and 350; LAMB, LARS, Lion and Muon drop to about 10⁻⁴ within 200 to 400 steps, stay in a noisy band between 10⁻⁴ and 10⁻⁷ until step 1300, and fall to about 10⁻⁹ to 10⁻¹⁰ as the learning rate reaches zero. Right: Adam's and Adafactor's relative update fall to the floor of 10⁻¹² by steps 500 and 250; LAMB, Lion and Muon stay near 10⁻² until the end, LARS a little lower and noisier, and all four fall to about 10⁻⁴ at step 1500.)

All the original checks hold. LAMB's relative update norm is 0.100000 and LARS's 0.010000. Lion ends at \(w=0.8\) with stored momentum 0.0905, and its update norm equals \(\eta\sqrt d\) to \(1.1\times10^{-16}\). Adafactor's six weights are all 0.95, its row moments 14/3 and 56/3 and its column moments 2.5, 10 and 22.5. All 12 zero-gradient cases stay exactly at zero. For a \(256\times128\) matrix, Adafactor stores 384 floats against 65536 for Adam and LAMB and 32768 for LARS and Lion.

The matrix fit separates two groups. Adam and Adafactor shrink their steps as the gradient shrinks: Adam's loss is \(1.3\times10^{-23}\) after 500 steps and Adafactor's exactly 0. LAMB, LARS, Lion and Muon take steps of a fixed size relative to the weights, as their update rules prescribe, so they cannot settle while the learning rate is large. After 500 steps their losses are between \(7.5\times10^{-6}\) and \(1.1\times10^{-4}\), LAMB's higher than after 100 steps. They reach \(2.1\times10^{-9}\) (LARS) to \(8.2\times10^{-11}\) (LAMB) only because the learning rate decays to zero. All six end below the original example's threshold of \(10^{-5}\).

> Checking the theory

>> Newton–Schulz, Lookahead, Adafactor's factorisation and SAM

The program applies both Newton–Schulz polynomials to a \(64\times32\) Gaussian matrix for 1 to 20 steps. It compares the singular values with \(p^{(k)}\) and the result with the exact polar factor \(UV^\top\) from the SVD.
It runs gradient descent with Lookahead (\(\alpha=0.5\), \(k=5\) and 6) on \(\tfrac12hw^2\) for six outer cycles and compares the rate with the formula.
It takes one Adafactor step on \(64\times32\) gradients of rank 1 to 32 and compares \(\hat V\) built from the library's slots with \(G\circ G\).
Finally, it runs SAM with gradient descent (\(\eta=0.01\), 2000 steps) from 24 evenly spaced starting points, for five radii \(\rho\), on \([-3,3]\) on the loss

$$
L(w)=-e^{-(w+1)^2/(2\cdot0.08^2)}-0.8\,e^{-(w-1.5)^2/(2\cdot0.6^2)}+0.02\,w^2,
$$
\alttext(L of w equals minus e to the minus w plus 1 squared over 2 times 0.08 squared, minus 0.8 e to the minus w minus 1.5 squared over 2 times 0.6 squared, plus 0.02 w squared.)

which has a deep, sharp well at \(w=-1\) and a shallower, wide one at \(w=1.5\).

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/advanced_theory.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Tensor;
using Kind = nn::OptimizerKind;
using dm::graphics::RGB;
const auto F64 = nn::DType::Float64;

Tensor gauss(std::int64_t r, std::int64_t c, std::uint64_t seed) {
  std::mt19937_64 rng(seed);
  std::normal_distribution<double> N;
  std::vector<double> v(static_cast<std::size_t>(r * c));
  for (auto& x : v) x = N(rng);
  return nn::from_vector(v, {r, c});
}
Tensor slot(const nn::Optimizer& o, const std::string& name) {
  for (const auto& [k, v] : o.state()) if (k == name) return v;
  throw std::runtime_error("no slot " + name);
}

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(2, 2);

  // (a) Newton–Schulz on a 64×32 Gaussian matrix: singular values of the output after k steps, and the distance to the
  // exact polar factor U V^T from the SVD.
  const Tensor G = gauss(64, 32, 7);
  const auto d = nn::svd(G);
  const Tensor polar = nn::matmul(d.u, d.v, false, true);
  const auto s = d.s.to_vector<double>();
  const double fro = std::sqrt(nn::sum(nn::square(G)).item());
  std::print("64×32 Gaussian matrix: σ_max/‖G‖_F = {:.4f}, σ_min/‖G‖_F = {:.4f}.\n\n", s.front() / fro, s.back() / fro);
  std::print("| steps k | cubic: σ range of the output | cubic: ‖X − UVᵀ‖_F/‖UVᵀ‖_F | quintic: σ range | quintic: ‖X − UVᵀ‖_F/‖UVᵀ‖_F |\n| ---: | :--- | ---: | :--- | ---: |\n");
  const double pnorm = std::sqrt(nn::sum(nn::square(polar)).item());
  for (int k : {1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 12, 20}) {
    std::print("| {} |", k);
    for (auto c : {nn::NewtonSchulzCoefficients::cubic(), nn::NewtonSchulzCoefficients::quintic()}) {
      const Tensor X = nn::newton_schulz_orthogonalize(G, k, c);
      const auto sx = nn::svd(X).s.to_vector<double>();
      std::print(" {:.3f} – {:.3f} | {:.2e} |", *std::ranges::min_element(sx), *std::ranges::max_element(sx), std::sqrt(nn::sum(nn::square(X - polar)).item()) / pnorm);
    }
    std::print("\n");
  }
  {
    auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    auto iterate = [](double x, int k, nn::NewtonSchulzCoefficients c) {
      for (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) x = c.a * x + c.b * x * x * x + c.c * x * x * x * x * x;
      return x;
    };
    std::vector<double> xs, yc, yq;
    for (double x = 0; x <= 1.0001; x += 0.0005) xs.push_back(x), yc.push_back(iterate(x, 5, nn::NewtonSchulzCoefficients::cubic())), yq.push_back(iterate(x, 5, nn::NewtonSchulzCoefficients::quintic()));
    std::vector<double> px, pc, pq;
    const auto sc = nn::svd(nn::newton_schulz_orthogonalize(G, 5, nn::NewtonSchulzCoefficients::cubic())).s.to_vector<double>();
    const auto sq = nn::svd(nn::newton_schulz_orthogonalize(G, 5)).s.to_vector<double>();
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < s.size(); ++i) px.push_back(s[i] / fro), pc.push_back(sc[i]), pq.push_back(sq[i]);
    pl.lines(xs, yc, "cubic p⁽⁵⁾", RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2).lines(xs, yq, "quintic p⁽⁵⁾", RGB{220, 38, 38}, 2)
        .points(px, pc, "cubic, measured", RGB{37, 99, 235}, 5).points(px, pq, "quintic, measured", RGB{220, 38, 38}, 5)
        .abline_h(1.0, RGB{148, 163, 184}, 1)
        .x_limits(0, 1).y_limits(0, 1.9).y_ticks({0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1.0, 1.25}, {"0", "0.25", "0.5", "0.75", "1", "1.25"})
        .title("Newton–Schulz, 5 steps").x_label("input singular value σ / ‖G‖_F").y_label("output singular value");
    fig.add(pl);
  }

  // (b) Lookahead around gradient descent on 0.5 h w²: per-inner-step rate |1 − α + α c^k|^(1/k) with c = 1 − η h,
  // against |c| for plain gradient descent. Points: measured with the library over 6 outer cycles.
  {
    auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    std::vector<double> xs, gd, l5, l6;
    for (double eh = 0.005; eh <= 2.3; eh += 0.005) {
      const double c = 1 - eh;
      xs.push_back(eh), gd.push_back(std::abs(c));
      l5.push_back(std::pow(std::abs(0.5 + 0.5 * std::pow(c, 5)), 0.2));
      l6.push_back(std::pow(std::abs(0.5 + 0.5 * std::pow(c, 6)), 1.0 / 6));
    }
    std::vector<double> mx, m5, m6;
    double worst = 0;
    std::print("\nLookahead (α = 0.5) around gradient descent on 0.5·h·w², rate per inner step:\n\n| η·h | gradient descent | k = 5 formula | k = 5 measured | k = 6 formula | k = 6 measured |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (double eh : {0.1, 0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 1.8, 1.95, 2.1}) {
      std::vector<double> rates;
      for (int k : {5, 6}) {
        Tensor w = nn::from_vector(std::vector<double>{1.0}, {1});
        w.requires_grad_();
        nn::OptimizerConfig cfg;
        cfg.kind = Kind::Sgd;
        nn::Optimizer inner({w}, eh, cfg);
        nn::Lookahead la({w}, k, 0.5);
        for (int i = 0; i < 6 * k; ++i) inner.step({w.detach()}), la.after_step();
        rates.push_back(std::pow(std::abs(w.item()), 1.0 / (6 * k)));
      }
      const double c = 1 - eh, f5 = std::pow(std::abs(0.5 + 0.5 * std::pow(c, 5)), 0.2), f6 = std::pow(std::abs(0.5 + 0.5 * std::pow(c, 6)), 1.0 / 6);
      worst = std::max({worst, std::abs(rates[0] - f5), std::abs(rates[1] - f6)});
      std::print("| {} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} |\n", eh, std::abs(c), f5, rates[0], f6, rates[1]);
      mx.push_back(eh), m5.push_back(rates[0]), m6.push_back(rates[1]);
    }
    std::print("\nLargest difference between measured and formula: {:.1e}.\n", worst);
    pl.lines(xs, gd, "gradient descent", RGB{100, 116, 139}, 2).lines(xs, l5, "Lookahead k = 5", RGB{220, 38, 38}, 2).lines(xs, l6, "Lookahead k = 6", RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2)
        .points(mx, m5, RGB{220, 38, 38}, 6).points(mx, m6, RGB{37, 99, 235}, 6)
        .abline_h(1.0, RGB{148, 163, 184}, 1)
        .x_limits(0, 2.3).y_limits(0, 1.8).y_ticks({0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1.0, 1.25}, {"0", "0.25", "0.5", "0.75", "1", "1.25"})
        .title("Lookahead (α = 0.5) on a quadratic").x_label("η · h").y_label("error factor per inner step");
    fig.add(pl);
  }

  // (c) Adafactor's factored second moment R Cᵀ / mean(R) against G∘G for 64×32 gradients of rank r. After the first step
  // the slots hold exactly the row and column means of G∘G (+1e-30).
  {
    std::print("\n| rank of G | rank of G∘G (numerical) | relative error ‖V̂ − G∘G‖_F / ‖G∘G‖_F |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    std::vector<double> rx, ry;
    for (int r : {1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 12, 16, 24, 32}) {
      Tensor g = nn::matmul(gauss(64, r, 100 + r), gauss(r, 32, 200 + r));
      Tensor w = nn::zeros({64, 32}, F64);
      w.requires_grad_();
      nn::OptimizerConfig cfg;
      cfg.kind = Kind::Adafactor;
      nn::Optimizer o({w}, 0.01, cfg);
      o.step({g});
      const Tensor R = nn::reshape(slot(o, "row/0"), {64, 1}), C = nn::reshape(slot(o, "col/0"), {1, 32});
      const Tensor V = nn::matmul(R, C) / nn::mean(R), V2 = nn::square(g);
      const double err = std::sqrt(nn::sum(nn::square(V - V2)).item() / nn::sum(nn::square(V2)).item());
      const auto sv = nn::svd(V2).s.to_vector<double>();
      int rank = 0;
      for (double x : sv) rank += x > 1e-10 * sv[0];
      std::print("| {} | {} | {:.2e} |\n", r, rank, err);
      rx.push_back(r), ry.push_back(err);
    }
    auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    pl.lines(rx, ry, RGB{124, 58, 237}, 2).points(rx, ry, RGB{124, 58, 237}, 6)
        .x_limits(0, 33).y_limits(-0.05, 1.2).title("Adafactor's rank-one second moment").x_label("rank of the gradient G (64×32)").y_label("relative error of R Cᵀ / mean(R)");
    fig.add(pl);
  }

  // (d) SAM on a 1-D loss with a sharp deep well at w = −1 and a wide shallow one at w = 1.5. 24 starts on [−3, 3],
  // 2000 gradient-descent steps (η = 0.01) on the SAM gradients for several radii ρ.
  auto L = [](const Tensor& w) {
    return nn::sum(-1.0 * nn::exp(-1.0 * nn::square(w + 1.0) / (2 * 0.08 * 0.08)) - 0.8 * nn::exp(-1.0 * nn::square(w - 1.5) / (2 * 0.6 * 0.6)) + 0.02 * nn::square(w));
  };
  auto Ld = [&](double x) { return L(nn::from_vector(std::vector<double>{x}, {1})).item(); };
  std::print("\nLoss at the sharp minimum ≈ {:.4f}, at the flat one ≈ {:.4f}.\n\n| ρ | flat well (w within 0.5 of 1.5) | sharp well (w within 0.3 of −1, not at −1 + ρ) | at w = −1 + ρ (within 0.01) | elsewhere |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n", Ld(-1.0), Ld(1.5));
  const std::vector<double> radii{0.0, 0.2, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7};
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> finals(radii.size());
  std::vector<double> starts;
  for (int i = 0; i < 24; ++i) starts.push_back(-3.0 + 6.0 * (i + 0.5) / 24);
  for (std::size_t r = 0; r < radii.size(); ++r) {
    const double rho = radii[r];
    int flat = 0, sharp = 0, shadow = 0, other = 0;
    for (double w0 : starts) {
      Tensor w = nn::from_vector(std::vector<double>{w0}, {1});
      w.requires_grad_();
      nn::OptimizerConfig cfg;
      cfg.kind = Kind::Sgd;
      nn::Optimizer o({w}, 0.01, cfg);
      for (int t = 0; t < 2000; ++t) o.step(nn::sam_gradients({w}, [&] { return L(w); }, rho).gradients);
      const double x = w.item();
      finals[r].push_back(x);
      if (std::abs(x - 1.5) < 0.5) ++flat;
      else if (rho > 0 && std::abs(x - (-1 + rho)) < 0.01) ++shadow;
      else if (std::abs(x + 1) < 0.3) ++sharp;
      else ++other;
    }
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", rho, flat, sharp, shadow, other);
  }
  {
    auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    const std::vector<std::size_t> shown{0, 2, 3};
    const std::vector<RGB> cols{{37, 99, 235}, {22, 163, 74}, {220, 38, 38}};
    const std::vector<double> sizes{7, 5, 3};
    for (std::size_t j = 0; j < shown.size(); ++j)
      pl.points(starts, finals[shown[j]], std::format("ρ = {}", radii[shown[j]]), cols[j], sizes[j]);
    pl.abline_h(-1.0, RGB{148, 163, 184}, 1).abline_h(1.5, RGB{148, 163, 184}, 1)
        .x_limits(-3, 3).y_limits(-1.6, 3.4).y_ticks({-1.5, -1, -0.5, 0, 0.5, 1, 1.5, 2}, {"−1.5", "−1", "−0.5", "0", "0.5", "1", "1.5", "2"})
        .title("SAM: where gradient descent ends").x_label("starting point w₀").y_label("w after 2000 steps");
    fig.add(pl);
  }
  fig.size(1100, 1000).fit_panels();
  fig.save("build/advanced_theory.svg");
}
```
