// result_end

\caption(Top left: \(p^{(5)}\) for the cubic and quintic polynomials (lines) and the singular values of the library's output for a \(64\times32\) Gaussian matrix (points). Top right: Lookahead's error factor per inner step on a quadratic, from the formula (lines) and measured (points), with gradient descent's \(|1-\eta h|\). Bottom left: relative error of Adafactor's rank-one second moment against the rank of the gradient. Bottom right: SAM; the final position against the starting point for three radii (grey lines at the two minima).)
\alttext(Top left: the blue cubic curve rises from 0 and reaches 1 near σ = 0.3; the red quintic curve rises steeply, then oscillates between about 0.7 and 1.15 for all larger inputs; the measured points, at inputs between 0.06 and 0.28, lie on the curves. Top right: gradient descent's grey V touches 0 at η·h = 1 and passes 1 at 2; both Lookahead curves stay between 0.86 and 0.96 up to η·h = 1.5; beyond that the k = 6 curve rises past 1 near 2, while the k = 5 curve dips to 0 at η·h = 2 and is still 0.79 at 2.1. Bottom left: the error is 0 at rank 1, jumps to 0.55 at rank 2 and rises slowly to 0.78 at rank 32. Bottom right: starts right of about −0.7 end at 1.5 (right of −0.5 for ρ = 0.3 and −0.2 for ρ = 0.5); for ρ = 0 the starts on the left end at −1; for ρ = 0.3 the two starts near −0.9 and −0.6 end at −0.7, and for ρ = 0.5 starts between −2.1 and −0.4 end at −0.5.)

The singular values of the input lie between 0.060 and 0.280 times its Frobenius norm. The cubic iteration brings them to 0.432–0.992 after five steps, to 0.944–1.000 after eight, and to the polar factor itself after twelve, with relative error \(1.5\times10^{-15}\). The quintic brings them to 0.668–1.202 after two steps and then stays at 0.682–1.134, 0.19–0.21 from the polar factor. Muon needs only singular values of order one, so the quintic gets there in two steps where the cubic needs eight. Both curves pass through the measured points, as the derivation requires.

Lookahead's measured rates agree with \(|1-\alpha+\alpha c^k|^{1/k}\) to \(1.1\times10^{-16}\). Where gradient descent is well tuned, Lookahead is slower: at \(\eta h=1\) gradient descent converges in one step, while Lookahead only halves the error every cycle, a rate of \(0.5^{1/5}=0.8706\) per step. Near the stability limit the odd \(k\) helps. At \(\eta h=1.95\) the rate is 0.647 for \(k=5\) against 0.95 for gradient descent. At \(\eta h=2.1\), where gradient descent diverges with factor 1.1, Lookahead with \(k=5\) still converges with rate 0.789; with \(k=6\) it diverges (1.056).

Adafactor's factored moment is exact for a rank-one gradient, with relative error \(1.8\times10^{-16}\). The numerical rank of \(G\circ G\) is 3, 6, 10 and 21 for ranks 2, 3, 4 and 6, exactly \(r(r+1)/2\), and 32 from rank 8 on. The error is already 0.55 at rank two and rises to 0.78 at full rank. Adafactor's per-coordinate scale is therefore a coarse approximation for typical gradients, which have rank above one even for a single example.

SAM does not lead runs to the flat minimum, which lies 0.23 higher than the sharp one. With \(\rho=0\), 15 of 24 starts end in the flat well and 9 in the sharp one. With \(\rho=0.2\), 14 and 10; with \(\rho=0.7\), 12 end in the flat well (one run ends outside both wells). Larger radii send slightly fewer starts to the flat well, not more.
What changes is the sharp well. From \(\rho=0.3\) on, runs stop at exactly \(w=-1+\rho\): 2 runs at \(\rho=0.3\), 7 at 0.5 and 9 at 0.7. At that point the loss slopes down to the right, so the ascent step \(\epsilon=-\rho\) lands on the sharp minimum, where the gradient pushes back from either side. For \(\rho\le0.2\) no run stops there; the point \(-1+\rho\) is then still on the sharp well's wall, where the ascent step points the other way. SAM replaces the sharp minimum by a shadow at distance \(\rho\) rather than removing it. In one dimension its first-order ascent step cannot see a minimum farther away than \(\rho\).

> A dataset: eight methods on digits

The digits data (Alpaydin and Kaynak, 1998) has 1797 handwritten digits of \(8\times8\) pixels, scaled to \([0,1]\). Here the first 1137 train, the next 300 validate and the last 360 test. An MLP with one hidden ReLU layer of 128 units is trained for 500 batches of 64 at learning rates from 0.001 to 0.3, for two seeds (a short run that keeps this sweep of 96 fits to a few seconds). The methods are Adam, LAMB, LARS (trust coefficient 0.1), Lion, Adafactor (default relative step and parameter scaling) and Muon, plus Adam with Lookahead (\(k=5\), \(\alpha=0.5\)) and Adam with SAM (\(\rho=0.05\)). Muon also updates the biases, with plain momentum. The rate with the best mean validation accuracy is chosen.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/advanced_digits.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Tensor;
using Kind = nn::OptimizerKind;
using dm::graphics::RGB;
const auto F32 = nn::DType::Float32;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits();
  const std::int64_t n = 1797, n_train = 1137, n_val = 300, n_test = n - n_train - n_val;
  std::vector<float> px(static_cast<std::size_t>(n * 64));
  for (int j = 0; j < 64; ++j) {
    const auto col = digits.get_column<double>(std::format("pixel_{}", j)).values();
    for (std::int64_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) px[i * 64 + j] = static_cast<float>(col[i] / 16.0);
  }
  const Tensor X = nn::from_vector(px, {n, 64});
  const Tensor y = nn::cast(nn::from_vector(digits.get_column<double>("digit").values(), {n}), nn::DType::Int64);
  const Tensor Xtr = nn::slice(X, {0, 0}, {n_train, 64}), ytr = nn::slice(y, {0}, {n_train});
  const Tensor Xva = nn::slice(X, {n_train, 0}, {n_val, 64}), yva = nn::slice(y, {n_train}, {n_val});
  const Tensor Xte = nn::slice(X, {n_train + n_val, 0}, {n_test, 64}), yte = nn::slice(y, {n_train + n_val}, {n_test});

  const std::vector<std::string> names{"Adam", "LAMB", "LARS", "Lion", "Adafactor", "Muon", "Adam+Lookahead", "Adam+SAM"};
  const std::vector<RGB> colors{{37, 99, 235}, {220, 38, 38}, {202, 138, 4}, {22, 163, 74}, {124, 58, 237}, {17, 24, 39}, {14, 165, 233}, {219, 39, 119}};
  const std::vector<double> rates{1e-3, 3e-3, 1e-2, 3e-2, 1e-1, 3e-1};
  const int steps = 500;
  const std::vector<std::uint64_t> seeds{1, 2};
  const double ns = static_cast<double>(seeds.size());
  std::print("MLP 64-128-10 (ReLU), {} training, {} validation and {} test images, {} steps of batch 64, {} seeds.\n"
             "LAMB, LARS and Lion with weight decay 0; LARS trust coefficient 0.1; Adafactor with its default relative step and parameter scaling;\n"
             "Lookahead k = 5, α = 0.5; SAM ρ = 0.05.\n\n", n_train, n_val, n_test, steps, seeds.size());
  std::print("| method | val. acc. η = 0.001 | 0.003 | 0.01 | 0.03 | 0.1 | 0.3 | chosen η | test accuracy | final training loss | seconds per run |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> val(names.size()), best_curve(names.size());
  for (std::size_t r = 0; r < names.size(); ++r) {
    double best_val = -1, best_test = 0, best_loss = 0, best_rate = 0, seconds = 0;
    for (double rate : rates) {
      double va = 0, te = 0, fl = 0;
      std::vector<double> curve(steps / 50, 0.0);
      for (std::uint64_t seed : seeds) {
        const auto t0 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
        nn::ParameterStore p(seed, F32);
        auto f = [&](const Tensor& x) {
          return nn::dense(p, "out", nn::dense(p, "h", x, 128, {.activation = nn::Activation::Relu, .init = nn::init::he_normal()}), 10);
        };
        { nn::NoGradGuard ng; (void)f(nn::slice(Xtr, {0, 0}, {2, 64})); }
        const auto params = p.parameters();
        std::shared_ptr<nn::Optimizer> opt;
        std::shared_ptr<nn::Muon> muon;
        std::shared_ptr<nn::Lookahead> look;
        if (names[r] == "Muon") {
          muon = std::make_shared<nn::Muon>(params, rate);
        } else {
          nn::OptimizerConfig cfg;
          cfg.kind = names[r] == "LAMB" ? Kind::Lamb : names[r] == "LARS" ? Kind::Lars : names[r] == "Lion" ? Kind::Lion : names[r] == "Adafactor" ? Kind::Adafactor : Kind::Adam;
          cfg.trust_coefficient = 0.1;
          opt = std::make_shared<nn::Optimizer>(params, rate, cfg);
          if (names[r] == "Adam+Lookahead") look = std::make_shared<nn::Lookahead>(params, 5, 0.5);
        }
        std::mt19937_64 rng(seed);
        std::vector<std::int64_t> order(n_train);
        std::iota(order.begin(), order.end(), 0);
        std::size_t pos = n_train;
        double running = 0;
        for (int s = 1; s <= steps; ++s) {
          if (pos + 64 > order.size()) std::shuffle(order.begin(), order.end(), rng), pos = 0;
          const Tensor idx = nn::from_vector(std::vector<std::int64_t>(order.begin() + pos, order.begin() + pos + 64), {64});
          pos += 64;
          const Tensor xb = nn::gather(Xtr, idx, 0), yb = nn::gather(ytr, idx, 0);
          auto loss_fn = [&] { return nn::sparse_softmax_cross_entropy(f(xb), yb); };
          if (names[r] == "Adam+SAM") {
            const auto sam = nn::sam_gradients(params, loss_fn, 0.05);
            running += sam.loss.item() / 50;
            opt->step(sam.gradients);
          } else {
            const Tensor loss = loss_fn();
            running += loss.item() / 50;
            if (muon) muon->minimize(loss); else opt->minimize(loss);
            if (look) look->after_step();
          }
          if (s % 50 == 0) curve[s / 50 - 1] += running / ns, running = 0;
        }
        nn::NoGradGuard ng;
        auto acc = [&](const Tensor& Xs, const Tensor& ys) { return nn::mean(nn::cast(nn::equal(nn::argmax(f(Xs), -1), ys), F32)).item(); };
        va += acc(Xva, yva) / ns, te += acc(Xte, yte) / ns;
        fl += nn::sparse_softmax_cross_entropy(f(Xtr), ytr).item() / ns;
        seconds += std::chrono::duration<double>(std::chrono::steady_clock::now() - t0).count() / (ns * rates.size());
      }
      val[r].push_back(va);
      if (va > best_val) best_val = va, best_test = te, best_loss = fl, best_rate = rate, best_curve[r] = curve;
    }
    std::print("| {} |", names[r]);
    for (double v : val[r]) std::print(" {:.1f}% |", 100 * v);
    std::print(" {} | {:.1f}% | {:.4f} | {:.2f} |\n", best_rate, 100 * best_test, best_loss, seconds);
  }

  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  {
    auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    std::vector<double> xs;
    for (double rate : rates) xs.push_back(std::log10(rate));
    for (std::size_t r = 0; r < names.size(); ++r) {
      std::vector<double> xv, ys;
      for (std::size_t k = 0; k < rates.size(); ++k)
        if (100 * val[r][k] >= 88) xv.push_back(xs[k]), ys.push_back(100 * val[r][k]);  // runs below 88% are left out
      pl.lines(xv, ys, colors[r], 2).points(xv, ys, colors[r], 5);  // colours as in the legend on the right
    }
    pl.x_limits(-3.1, -0.4).x_ticks(xs, {"0.001", "0.003", "0.01", "0.03", "0.1", "0.3"}).y_limits(88, 98.5)
        .y_ticks({88, 90, 92, 94, 96, 98}, {"88", "90", "92", "94", "96", "98"})
        .title("Validation accuracy by learning rate").x_label("learning rate η (log scale)").y_label("validation accuracy (%)");
    fig.add(pl);
  }
  {
    auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    double lowest = 0;
    for (const auto& c : best_curve) for (double v : c) lowest = std::min(lowest, std::log10(v));
    for (std::size_t r = 0; r < names.size(); ++r) {
      std::vector<double> xs, ys;
      for (std::size_t k = 0; k < best_curve[r].size(); ++k) xs.push_back(50.0 * (k + 1)), ys.push_back(std::log10(best_curve[r][k]));
      pl.lines(xs, ys, names[r], colors[r], 1.5);
    }
    pl.x_limits(0, steps * 1.02).y_limits(std::floor(lowest) - 0.2, 7).title("Training loss at the chosen rate").x_label("training step").y_label("log10 cross-entropy (mean of 50 batches)");
    fig.add(pl);
  }
  fig.size(1100, 540).fit_panels();
  fig.save("build/advanced_digits.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
MLP 64-128-10 (ReLU), 1137 training, 300 validation and 360 test images, 500 steps of batch 64, 2 seeds.
LAMB, LARS and Lion with weight decay 0; LARS trust coefficient 0.1; Adafactor with its default relative step and parameter scaling;
Lookahead k = 5, α = 0.5; SAM ρ = 0.05.

| method | val. acc. η = 0.001 | 0.003 | 0.01 | 0.03 | 0.1 | 0.3 | chosen η | test accuracy | final training loss | seconds per run |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Adam | 96.0% | 96.8% | 97.0% | 97.2% | 94.2% | 69.0% | 0.03 | 90.6% | 0.0005 | 0.07 |
| LAMB | 94.3% | 97.0% | 96.2% | 9.5% | 10.0% | 10.0% | 0.003 | 90.1% | 0.0153 | 0.08 |
| LARS | 91.0% | 94.7% | 96.0% | 93.8% | 93.8% | 9.7% | 0.01 | 89.0% | 0.0225 | 0.06 |
| Lion | 94.5% | 95.2% | 94.7% | 94.3% | 39.7% | 9.7% | 0.003 | 88.5% | 0.0001 | 0.05 |
| Adafactor | 90.8% | 95.2% | 96.5% | 96.5% | 98.0% | 97.3% | 0.1 | 91.1% | 0.0006 | 0.08 |
| Muon | 92.0% | 95.8% | 96.0% | 93.8% | 93.7% | 95.5% | 0.01 | 91.7% | 0.0000 | 0.20 |
| Adam+Lookahead | 94.5% | 96.7% | 97.2% | 97.3% | 96.5% | 86.8% | 0.03 | 90.6% | 0.0014 | 0.06 |
| Adam+SAM | 95.8% | 96.8% | 96.8% | 97.3% | 95.3% | 33.5% | 0.03 | 90.7% | 0.0003 | 0.11 |
\image(figures/advanced_digits.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Left: validation accuracy (mean of two seeds) against the learning rate, in the colours of the legend on the right; runs below 88% are left out. Right: training loss at each method's chosen rate (mean over 50 batches, log scale).)
\alttext(Left: Adafactor's purple line rises from 90.8% at 0.001 to 98.0% at 0.1 and is still 97.3% at 0.3; Muon's black line stays between 92.0 and 96.0% at all six rates; Adam, Adam with Lookahead and Adam with SAM stay near 97% from 0.003 to 0.03 and then drop; LAMB's red line peaks at 97.0% at 0.003 and leaves the plot from 0.03; LARS's line rises from 91.0% to 96.0% at 0.01 and stays at 93.8% up to 0.1; Lion's green line stays between 94.3 and 95.2% up to 0.03. Right: Muon's black line falls steadily to about 10⁻⁵·⁴; Adam, Lion, Adafactor, Adam with Lookahead and Adam with SAM end between 10⁻²·⁸ and 10⁻³·⁵; LAMB and LARS end near 10⁻¹·⁶.)

At their best rates the methods reach 95.2% (Lion) to 98.0% (Adafactor) validation accuracy and 88.5–91.7% test accuracy. With 300 validation images, one image is 0.33 points and the standard error near 97% is about one point, so most of these differences are within two standard errors; only the extremes, Adafactor and Lion, are clearly apart. Test accuracy is lower than validation accuracy for every method, as in the report on adaptive optimizers; the last 360 images seem to be harder.

The methods differ most in their sensitivity to the learning rate. Adafactor ranges from 90.8% at 0.001 to 98.0% at 0.1 and keeps 97.3% at 0.3, because its rate is capped at \(1/\sqrt t\) and scaled by the weights' RMS; in only 500 steps its smallest rates have not yet converged. Muon stays between 92.0 and 96.0%, because its matrix updates have a fixed spectral size whatever the gradient's scale. Adam keeps 94.2% at 0.1 and drops to 69.0% at 0.3; Lookahead holds it at 86.8% there and SAM falls to 33.5%. The fixed-size updates of LAMB, LARS and Lion fail at large rates: LAMB is at chance (10%) from 0.03, Lion falls to 39.7% at 0.1 and to chance at 0.3, LARS to chance at 0.3.
At the chosen rate Muon drives the batch loss lowest, to about \(10^{-5.4}\) after 500 steps, and LAMB and LARS leave the highest, near \(10^{-1.6}\) (final training loss 0.015 and 0.023). A Muon run took about three times as long as an Adam run and a SAM run about twice as long (last column; timings vary between runs). SAM needs two gradient evaluations per step, and Muon's five Newton–Schulz steps cost more than this small network's forward and backward pass.

> Conclusion

The four built-in rules implement their definitions exactly, and the additions follow their derivations to machine precision. Newton–Schulz iteration is a polynomial in the singular values: the cubic gives the exact orthogonal factor slowly, while Muon's quintic gives an approximately orthogonal one in two steps and no better afterwards. Lookahead's effect on a quadratic is the closed form \(1-\alpha+\alpha c^k\), which slows a tuned optimizer but stabilises an oscillating one when \(k\) is odd. Adafactor's factorisation is exact only for rank-one squared gradients, and a rank-two gradient already gives a relative error of 0.55.

Two results run against common descriptions. Optimizers with fixed relative steps, LAMB, LARS, Lion and Muon, cannot converge at a constant learning rate and need the rate to decay. In one dimension SAM did not find the flat minimum; it moved the sharp minimum's attractor to a shadow point a distance \(\rho\) away.
On digits every method reached nearly the same accuracy, and the practical difference was robustness to the learning rate, where Adafactor and Muon stood out.

